            "name": "San Francisco",
            "@type": "City"
          }
        ],
        "articleBody": "Knowing when to call in the pros for your website’s JSON-LD schema can really make a difference in your AI visibility. Here are some signals that it might be time to bring in professional help:\n\n*   **You have a large or complex site:** If your website has many different page types—like blogs, product pages, service listings, or location pages—designing and templating schema across all of them can get overwhelming. A professional can help ensure consistency and accuracy, something that’s tough to maintain manually on a big site.\n\n*   **You want to be a trusted source for AI:** If your goal is to be frequently cited by Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, or other answer engines, you need highly detailed entity modeling. This involves thoroughly describing your organization, services, and key personnel. This strategic depth often requires expert knowledge to implement effectively.\n\n*   **Integrating schema with backend systems is challenging:** If you need your schema to dynamically pull data from your CMS, e-commerce platform, or knowledge graph, it requires development expertise to keep everything synchronized. Professionals can set up automated solutions that stay current as your data changes.\n\n*   **Your content types are complex:** Dealing with nuanced content like multi-step how-to guides, product bundles, or publications with multiple authors makes choosing the right schema types and properties complicated. An expert understands the intricacies and can model these complex relationships correctly, ensuring AI systems accurately interpret your valuable content.",
        "datePublished": "2026-07-19"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 19,
      "item": {
        "name": "What to Stop Doing If You Want More AI Citations",
        "step": [
          {
            "name": "Step 1",
            "text": "It's easy to get caught up in old habits when trying to get AI to recommend your business, but some common practices actually hurt your chances. First off, stop using vague marketing fluff. AI systems are looking for direct answers, not flowery language. If your content is full of buzzwords that don't actually explain what you do concisely, AI will likely skip right over it. Second, don't write super long answers without getting to the point quickly. AI loves a direct answer, and then you can elaborate. If you bury the key information, it's less likely to be extracted and cited. Third, avoid only targeting topics based on generic keyword tools. While keywords are still important for traditional search, AI is often looking for answers to specific customer questions. These questions might not always show up as high-volume keywords, but they're critical for AI citations. Finally, definitely don't skip out on structured data or schema markup. This tells AI exactly what your content is about and makes it much easier for it to understand and cite. If you're struggling to implement structured data effectively or identify those real customer questions, that's a good sign it's time to bring in professional help to ensure your technical content architecture is spot on. If you're ready to make your content AI-citation friendly, we can help transform your data into AI-citable content today.",
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "position": 1
          }
        ],
